it's the first trip to china by a u.n  human rights commissioner since 2005.  but details of michelle bachelet's visit  have been hard to come by  over six days she spent time in  guangzhou in china's south as well as  the cities of uramuchi and kashgar in  xinjiang province  due to strict coronavirus regulations no  reporters were allowed to travel with  her and many of her meetings including  with president xi jinping have taken  place online because for me  it is a priority to engage with the  government of china directly  to see how we can collaborate  on human rights issues domestically  regional and global  for development peace and security being  sustainable locally and across borders  human rights have to be at the core  activists say government controls have  prevented bachelet from clearly  assessing alleged atrocities taking  place against the uyghur people  her visit was first planned in 2018  following widespread allegations that  china was subjecting uigas to forced  labor sterilization and torture and  several governments and rights groups  say beijing is guilty of carrying out  genocide  i doubt she would have unfettered access  to  the region i doubt that she would be  able to talk to the victims of the  government's abuses  so you know i don't have high hopes  attention on xinjiang has intensified  this week following an unprecedented  leak of police files purporting to  reveal bleak conditions inside one of  the province's notorious internment  camps  thousands of mugshots and documents  including a speech ordering guards to  shoot and kill those who try to flee  challenge china's description of the  camps as voluntary vocational training  centers it's nothing to do with poverty  elevation  nothing to do with helping people to get  jobs it's about brutal brainwashing it's  about putting them in prison-like prison  camps guarded by heavily armed police  beijing has denied accusations of  mistreatment calling them the lie of the  century it says its policies in the  region are designed to stamp out  terrorism  xi jinping defended china's human rights  record on wednesday saying it would not  be patronized by other countries  michelle bachelet says her trip is not  an investigation but a mission to  promote human rights but critics fear  there's little it will achieve katrina  you al jazeera beijing
 

